{"reference":"John 16","book":"John","chapter":16,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions about interpretation","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Verses 1–4 anticipate exclusion from synagogues and even violence understood by its perpetrators as service to God. These statements have been used to reconstruct stages of conflict between the Johannine community and synagogues, but they do not themselves establish a single late-first-century decree or a uniform break. In verses 7–15, the Spirit of truth is described as teaching, reminding, testifying, exposing, guiding, and glorifying Jesus. Readers have understood these descriptions in overlapping ways—as personal presence, advocacy, communal memory, and in later Trinitarian readings—so they should not simply be treated as interchangeable.","verse_refs":["John 16:1-4","John 16:4-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_john_016_730cdeb8c36507d142422325"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}